Z||000011||DaRkLoRD||07-12-2001||07:37 PM||||I know what you're going through with your sister. my sister lost 2 friends less than a week apart, this year after christmas.. she was REALLY upset.. stopped eating, refused to talk to anyone, listening to one Moby song over and over again (Southside.. the one they played at the funeral...) just sat there shaking.... and sometimes she'd just start crying, and she wouldn't stop. I'm still worried about her, but she's slowly getting over this..<P>email me if you want to talk...<BR> <A HREF="mailto:steve@digitalfusion.on.ca">steve@digitalfusion.on.ca</A> <P>------------------<BR>     <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>     <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A><p>[This message has been edited by DaRkLoRD (edited 07-13-2001).]||||reg||9
Z||000012||AkursedX||07-12-2001||07:44 PM||||I'm sorry to hear....<P>You know, I would love to say that I couldn't possibly imagine what your sister is going through, but I can.  As I have mentioned before on this forum.  I lost my best friend/true love to suicide, and I lost another close friend about 3 or 4 weeks later.  And it's been over a year, and I still have not come anywhere near to recovering.  <P> I don't want to get into details about my tragedy.  But I want to say that you gotta be there for your sister.  And try as hard as you can to get her to open up, to talk about it.  I think my worst mistake is how I never, and still don't talk about it.  It eats away at me everyday.  But I think if she has good family, and friends, who will support her, and be there for her, I am more than certain you can help her get through this.  But it'll take time.  Time is the only thing that will truley be able to heal wounds as grave as those.  My best wises to you and your sister.<P>------------------<BR><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic2_2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Galen||07-12-2001||08:25 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cliff Pennock:<BR><B>It's things like this that makes you wonder what the world is coming to.<P>I hope they will find the killer and that they will lock him away for the rest of his pathetic life.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I agree completely.<P>edit: cuz I forgot to say anything<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Galen (edited 07-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||KnightRyder31||07-12-2001||09:35 PM||||Yeah, he was murdered by some little puke named Carl Graves Jr.<BR>Sad thing is, I doubt that he will even get 1st degree murder.<BR>Our system is very messed up!<BR>My cousins name was Robert Santee.<BR>He was great man.<BR>Did a lot for society, and was and still is very loved.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000015||EdsB52||07-13-2001||01:18 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by KnightRyder31:<BR><B>Yeah, he was murdered by some little puke named Carl Graves Jr.<BR>Sad thing is, I doubt that he will even get 1st degree murder.<BR>Our system is very messed up!<BR>My cousins name was Robert Santee.<BR>He was great man.<BR>Did a lot for society, and was and still is very loved.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Why is our system mesed up in your opinion? We all have due process rights, even if that person is a convicted felon. If indeed the named person is guilty and found that way, then he should spend the rest of his life in jail. But if if that person is not actually guilty, hence acquited, he should be released and exonerated. People are acquited and released years after a wrong conviction all the time, even when people (society)have/has convicted those people. A loss of a great man, such as your cousin, is not justification for an overzealous prosecution. ||||reg||1
Z||000016||Ken Wittlief||07-13-2001||11:07 AM||||FieroChic88<P>  there is no explaination or reason for what has happened.<P>  we live in a world now where we exist FAR above survival mode - almost everything we do is about enertainment, comfort, and personal pleasure<P>  a person can survive with one set of clothers, no shoes, a bowl of rice a day and about a quart of water - by comparison we all live like kings in this world and most of us dont even realize it.<P>  we have made incredible technology advances in the last few hundred years, but emotionally we are basically the same as we were 10,000 years ago - we are emotional beings with incredible power of destruction at our disposal.<P>   There is nothing anyone can do to make sense of what has happened - there will be no words of comfort to find a silver lining to this.<P>   But in a while when your sister is able to catch her breath - you might float the idea of taking the tragity that has happened and turn it into a cause of sorts - take what has happened and make it the inspiration to find a way to make the world a better place.<P>  On one hand we have the science and technology to bring an accident victim back from the brink of death, and to cure devistating illnesses, and we routinely spend millions of dollars to bring a single person back into good health<P> and on the other hand we have people walking around that will kill someone over a matter of $20.<P>   as a species we have a long way to go - I hope everyone that hears of this crime will take it to heart - and find a way to take us all towards a world in which this kind of stuff doesnt happen anymore.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Ken Wittlief||07-13-2001||11:14 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by KnightRyder31:<BR><B>My cousin was only 22 years old, and some mother F***er shot my cousin Robert, after he pushed this kid out of the way of the bullet.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>you must be incredibly proud of your cousin - "there is no greater love than to lay down your life for another"<P>  some people lay down there lives day by day for others, your cousin did it all at once.<P>  Your cousin is an honest to God HERO!<P>    and a Saint!<P>I hope as time goes by and you look back on that day, what you will remember is the golden heart your cousin had, and forget about the uglyness that took him away from you.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 07-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000018||84Bill||07-13-2001||11:40 AM||||No words can properly express how I feel so I won't even try. I can only hope that everyone touched by this tragedy can find some comfort and strength to get the through this terrible time. I'm not the God fearing type and I don't pray much but in this case I humbly beg for God to show mercy to the bereaved. <BR>My soul aches from the senselessness of it all, I hope your sister finds some solace no matter how elusive it may seem at this time.<BR>-Bill<p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 07-13-2001).]||||reg||9
Z||000019||KnightRyder31||07-13-2001||11:24 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR><B>  you must be incredibly proud of your cousin - "there is no greater love than to lay down your life for another"<P>  some people lay down there lives day by day for others, your cousin did it all at once.<P>  Your cousin is an honest to God HERO!<P>    and a Saint!<P>I hope as time goes by and you look back on that day, what you will remember is the golden heart your cousin had, and forget about the uglyness that took him away from you.<P>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 07-13-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thank you for the words you just gave me, that was very nice... and it means a lot to me. And the thing that really gets to me is... the kid that my cousin saved, is a piece of trash too! He is a convicted rapist!<BR>My cousin didn't even know this kid... what a wast. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000020||artherd||07-14-2001||04:46 AM||||I don't know if any of you remenber the "Poly Klass" tradjedy of a number of years ago, but she was a classmate of mine. I didn't know her terribly well, but her loss rattled us all to the core.<P>All I can say is be there for your sister. Show her Life, beauty, love, joy and happiness. SHow her that you love her, and that she can love you, and feel loved, and that love alone is immortal, everlasting. <P>KnightRider: your cousin is a hero, and an example we all admire, and indeed a true Hero to the entire human race.  If we could all be one tenth the Human Being he was, the world's problems would be over in the blink of an eye.<P>I just want you to know that though he may be gone, his life and love for a fellow human being live on, and continue to touch people, like me today, every day.<P>In the end, only kindness matters.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000021||AusFiero||07-17-2001||09:41 PM||||My deepest commiserations, I am deeply saddened by sensless acts of violence by animals like that.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> Yet as we know the law will give the perpertrator rights which he does not deserve. May the perp rot in hell. <P>I cannot offer much but please remember we are all here for you no matter what.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/fiero5.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A><BR>Need a Subwoofer Console or Speaker Door Pods?||||reg||1
Z||000022||The Fieromaster||07-17-2001||10:15 PM||||Its so hard for people to deal with these things when they happen.  Trust me i understand the feeling!  The afformentioned posts are correct though... Finding someone or a group of people with similar experiences to help her through her pain!!  Odly enough I myself felt allot better after I talked to someone who understood my loss (THANKS 84bill!!!!!).  Love is the best medicine and your sister is in my prayers!<P>------------------<BR>      <FONT COLOR="red">"Raced That, Wrecked Better"</FONT><BR>        <IMG SRC="http://www.askfelons.com/fieromaster/fieromastergif1.gif">        <BR><B>~In Memory Of Jesse Cesek~ 1980-2000</B><BR><FONT COLOR="blue"><B>AIM: Fieromaster</B></FONT><BR><B><FONT COLOR="purple">1985 GT ~ Purple, Black and Yellow Interior(Fastback Converted)</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="maroon">1997 Mitsu Eclipse GSX ~ Maroon w/ Hella Balls!(GSX = AWD, Turbo) DOA on 12/10/2000</FONT></B>||||reg||9
Z||000023||EdsB52||07-18-2001||12:00 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by AusFiero:<BR><B>Yet as we know the law will give the perpertrator rights which he does not deserve. May the perp rot in hell. <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>No argument there, except that with the US Constitution's 14th Amendment we have a thing called "equal protection." That provides a fair and equal trial for all accused, regardless of race, class, or status. Now we all know the rich are much more likely to be acquitted due to endless resource, but the Constitution cannot reference or protect against that, just the process. We all see the Jeffery Dahmer's that are caught with the smokin' gun, or the Collin Ferguson's (NY subway shooter) and ask why they even get a trial. The answer is that if they don't, then the accused innocent won't either because guilt or innocence is subjective, a fair trial is objective. Even with all of these Constitutional protections, most trials are not totally fair or without error, hence appealed. What I would hope for with individuals that are obviously guilty is a fair trial with competent counsel to prevent any appeals, instead of hoping for a horrible trial and an idiot for an attorney.<P>Remember, in America due process is across the board; what one defendant gets, so does another, it's assured through precedent. It's easy to be spiteful in situations like this, but harm done to due process is much more damaging to millions than harm done by giving an obviously guilty person a fair trial and chance for full or partial acquittal. What is paramount; protection of the government or protection from the government? Even though most think a crime done from one person to another is a crime against the victim, it's really a crime against the State. Hard to grasp for many, but the State bears the burden/luxury of disposing of this individual the way they want to. It's called 'prosecutorial discretion' and I think it sucks. It's actually the only fair way to do it though. If it were up to the victim to prosecute and that person had no recourse, the perpetrator wouldn't even stand trial. Conversely if the victim were loaded, an overzealous trial would take place and the defendant over prosecuted, hence no equal protection. I personally want to see more prosecutorial accountability, as they are now immune from even civil liability as police used to be and judges still are. I did hear of a case where a prosecutor did something obviously egregious and the courts were deciding if the plaintiff was allowed to sue. I don't know the outcome though. Even though we the people are allowed to sue doesn't mean that we will successfully, or that the judge will allow it to stand. Certain impunity from the citizens exists for State officials and I don't know why.||||reg||1
Z||000024||KnightRyder31||07-18-2001||06:26 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>I don't know if any of you remenber the "Poly Klass" tradjedy of a number of years ago, but she was a classmate of mine. I didn't know her terribly well, but her loss rattled us all to the core.<P>All I can say is be there for your sister. Show her Life, beauty, love, joy and happiness. SHow her that you love her, and that she can love you, and feel loved, and that love alone is immortal, everlasting. <P>KnightRider: your cousin is a hero, and an example we all admire, and indeed a true Hero to the entire human race.  If we could all be one tenth the Human Being he was, the world's problems would be over in the blink of an eye.<P>I just want you to know that though he may be gone, his life and love for a fellow human being live on, and continue to touch people, like me today, every day.<P>In the end, only kindness matters.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Once again;<BR>thank you for your kind words guys...<BR>it really means a lot to not only I, but my family as well. Thank you.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000025||Fierochic88||07-18-2001||06:30 PM||||Well guyz the police still don't have any firm leads.  They think it may be drug related..i.e. Shawn may have seen or known something he shouldn't have even though he didn't use drugs himself.   Hopefully they will find the bastard soon!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000026||EdsB52||07-18-2001||07:14 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by EdsB52:<BR><B> No argument there, except that with the US Constitution's 14th Amendment we have a thing called "equal protection." That provides a fair and equal trial for all accused, regardless of race, class, or status. Now we all know the rich are much more likely to be acquitted due to endless resource, but the Constitution cannot reference or protect against that, just the process. We all see the Jeffery Dahmer's that are caught with the smokin' gun, or the Collin Ferguson's (NY subway shooter) and ask why they even get a trial. The answer is that if they don't, then the accused innocent won't either because guilt or innocence is subjective, a fair trial is objective. Even with all of these Constitutional protections, most trials are not totally fair or without error, hence appealed. What I would hope for with individuals that are obviously guilty is a fair trial with competent counsel to prevent any appeals, instead of hoping for a horrible trial and an idiot for an attorney.<P>Remember, in America due process is across the board; what one defendant gets, so does another, it's assured through precedent. It's easy to be spiteful in situations like this, but harm done to due process is much more damaging to millions than harm done by giving an obviously guilty person a fair trial and chance for full or partial acquittal. What is paramount; protection of the government or protection from the government? Even though most think a crime done from one person to another is a crime against the victim, it's really a crime against the State. Hard to grasp for many, but the State bears the burden/luxury of disposing of this individual the way they want to. It's called 'prosecutorial discretion' and I think it sucks. It's actually the only fair way to do it though. If it were up to the victim to prosecute and that person had no recourse, the perpetrator wouldn't even stand trial. Conversely if the victim were loaded, an overzealous trial would take place and the defendant over prosecuted, hence no equal protection. I personally want to see more prosecutorial accountability, as they are now immune from even civil liability as police used to be and judges still are. I did hear of a case where a prosecutor did something obviously egregious and the courts were deciding if the plaintiff was allowed to sue. I don't know the outcome though. Even though we the people are allowed to sue doesn't mean that we will successfully, or that the judge will allow it to stand. Certain impunity from the citizens exists for State officials and I don't know why.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Once again, even the 'obviously guilty' deserve due process, and the 'probably innocent' deserve as much due process. Presumption of innocence rules.||||reg||1
Z||000027||FieroKyle||07-18-2001||11:56 PM||||I am always for if you take a life you give a life. The only suck thing about that sometimes innocent people get executed.||||reg||1
Z||000028||EdsB52||07-19-2001||06:37 PM||||You're referring to 'Old Testament' retribution, one of the primary arguments for pro-death penalty advocates. As you said, many innocent people will be executed. No official post mortem exonerations have been handed down yet (the US Supreme Court refuses to hear any cases that have to do with this area and relegate them as 'moot points'), as the government is just a little reluctant to expose an error that concluded in the murder of an innocent person. To be correct, the execution of a guilty person is still officially homicide (murder), but the majority of American society and the US Supreme Court are alright with that kind of murder. Some private groups have estimated the number of innocent people executed since 1977 (Gregg vs. Georgia reinstated capital punishment in 1976) at 26. That's about 1 per year or about a 3 1/2% margin of error. That sounds about right for most operations involving humans, and some uneducated people want to abbreviate the appeals process? Capital punishment is barbaric and irreversible, and if one error is made, there is no justification for it. For all people that think the US should continue to engage in capital punishment, ask yourself what the negligible number is for people to be executed and still justify death as a means of corrections/punishment/deterrence. In other words; if one person is accidentally executed over a ten year period, is that fine with you knowing that hundreds more guilty people were, in your words, "properly executed?" How about 1 per year? How about 2 per year? How about several per year? Where does your negligible threshold reside? I have the feeling I won't get any replies to this. Finally, Illinois has placed a moratorium on executions due to the number of people that were released from death row and from prison because they were wrongly convicted. Do we need the protection of the government, or protection from the government?||||reg||1
Z||000029||Steve Normington||07-19-2001||07:06 PM||||Personally, I don't care if it is life w/o parole or death. I just feel that their are some (people?) who should NEVER be out of prison.<P>PS. This isn't a response to EdsB52 since he doesn't expect any responses.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000030||EdsB52||07-19-2001||08:23 PM||||The fundamental goal is incapacitation for certain individuals. Extremists think it's necessary to kill people to incapacitate them. I agree, there are people that need to be removed from society, but is it necessary to kill them, especially when the risk of mistake looms large?<P>Actually I want responses and constructive arguments, I just don't expect any.||||reg||1
